+### Color Bleeding via SAT
+Stores RGB vectors in the SAT alongside extinction. Chosen when inter-reflection color transfer is required. [S2, unofficial]
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Transfer Function Changes:** The SAT must be updated whenever the Transfer Function changes because extinction coefficients are TF-dependent [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Shell Count:** If the radius of the ambient occlusion neighborhood $\Omega_A$ exceeds 10% of the dataset radius, more than three shells may be necessary to maintain image quality [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Bandwidth:** Storing RGB vectors for color bleeding increases memory bandwidth requirements compared to single-channel textures, though this was not observed to harm performance on the tested hardware [S1, unofficial].
+### Directional Soft Shadows via SAT
+Uses cuboid approximation of light cones. Chosen for soft shadows; static lights are cached, dynamic lights are queried per frame. [S1][S2, unofficial]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** SAT construction is $O(N \log N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, due to the recursive doubling technique [S1, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(1)$ per sample for lighting lookup, assuming static lights or pre-computed caches [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires two auxiliary 3D textures (SAT and Light Cache) of arbitrary size, independent of input volume resolution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   Head dataset (128x256x256, SAT 192^3, 15 shells): 111fps (ambient only), 143fps (dynamic illumination) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Engine dataset (256x256x128, SAT 64^3, 50 cone samples): 57fps (ambient only), 130fps (dynamic illumination) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Cornell box (256^3, SAT 256^3, 5 shells, 60 cone samples, static light): 133fps (ambient only), 161fps (static illumination) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Pelvis (512x512x461, SAT 64^3, 5 shells, 50 cone samples, dynamic light): 15fps (ambient only), 26fps (dynamic illumination) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Near-zero extra cost for static illumination (one additional texture lookup per sample). Interactive TF changes are supported due to fast SAT construction [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Dynamic illumination requires per-frame SAT queries. Approximation errors may occur if the number of shells is insufficient for large neighborhoods [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** SAT construction is $O(\log N)$ passes per dimension [S2, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(1)$ per sample for static effects (cache lookup) and $O(K)$ for dynamic shadows (K cuboid queries) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires two auxiliary 3D textures (SAT and Light Cache) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:**
+    *   Head (128x256x256, SAT 192^3, 15 shells): 111fps (AO only), 143fps (dynamic) [S2, unofficial].
+    *   Engine (256x256x128, SAT 64^3, 50 cone samples): 57fps (AO only), 130fps (dynamic) [S2, unofficial].
+    *   Cornell Box (256^3, SAT 256^3, 5 shells, 60 cone samples, static): 133fps (AO only), 161fps (static) [S2, unofficial].
+    *   Pelvis (512x512x461, SAT 64^3, 5 shells, 50 cone samples, dynamic): 15fps (AO only), 26fps (dynamic) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Interactive frame rates for global illumination effects; decouples lighting from view changes (AO); efficient constant-time neighborhood queries [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Memory intensive for high-resolution SATs; requires SAT rebuild on TF change; performance degrades with many light sources [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in the paper "Fast Shadows and Ambient Occlusion in GPU Volume Ray-Casting" by Diaz et al. [S1, unofficial]. It improves upon previous approaches by using extinction coefficients instead of opacity for the SAT and approximating neighborhoods with cuboid shells instead of eight adjacent octants, allowing for distance-based weighting [S1, unofficial].
+Developed by Schlegel et al. in 2011 [SMP11], this technique builds on earlier SAT-based ambient occlusion work by Desgranges and Engel [DE07] [S1]. It was introduced to provide interactive global illumination in volume ray casting by leveraging the order-independent summation property of extinction coefficients [S1]. It is used in scientific visualization and medical imaging where realistic lighting enhances depth perception [S1].
 
